UID:7b69db795b7a0c74@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260908T210659Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 9/8/2026: SJC invites comments on a proposed 
 amendment to Rule 4A of the Maine Rules of Unified Criminal Procedure to a
 llow consideration of a complaint certified on information and belief.  Co
 mments due by September 18\, 2026.
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nCOURT ALERTS IN EFFECT.\nSee Active Alerts\nHome\n
 →\nNews & Announcements\nSJC invites comments on a proposed amendment to
  Rule 4A of the Maine Rules of Unified Criminal Procedure to allow conside
 ration of a complaint certified on information and belief.  Comments due b
 y September 18\, 2026.\nDate:\n9/8/2026\nThe Maine Supreme Judicial Court 
 invites comments on a proposed amendment to Rule 4A of the Maine Rules of 
 Unified Criminal Procedure that would make the rule consistent with the 20
 25 amendment to M.R.U. Crim. P. 3(b) authorizing the attorney for the Stat
 e to sign a complaint and certify it on information and belief.\nThe propo
 sed amendments are available at the links below.\nAny comments must be fil
 ed with the Executive Clerk of the Supreme Judicial Court by\n4:00 p.m. on
  Friday\, September 18\, 2026\n.  Comments must be in an email sent to law
 court.clerk@courts.maine.gov.  If the comments are in an attachment to the
  email\, the attachment must be a document in native (text-based and not s
 canned) portable document format (.pdf).  The Clerk's Office will acknowle
 dge receipt of the email via reply email.\nAll comments must contain (1) t
 he name and mailing address of the individual submitting the comments and 
 (2) the name\, mailing address\, and primary telephone number of the organ
 ization (if any) on whose behalf the comments are submitted.  An individua
 l need not be an attorney to submit comments individually or on behalf of 
 an organization.\nComments are public documents and may be posted on the C
 ourts website.\nDated: September 8\, 2026\nMatthew Pollack\nExecutive Cler
 k\nMaine Supreme Judicial Court\n205 Newbury Street Room 139\nPortland\, M
 aine 04101\n(207) 822-4146\nSupporting documents\nProposed amendments\nPDF
  version of this notice\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id
 =13362193
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13362193
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:985bfbe13fd75e82@role-professional-responsibility.maine-government-fee
 ds
DTSTAMP:20260908T210659Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260908
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] 9/8/2026: SJC invites comments on proposed am
 endments to the Maine Rules of Evidence to permit parties to authenticate 
 certain electronic evidence other than through the testimony of a foundati
 on witness. Comments due by September 29\, 2026.
DESCRIPTION:Home\nSkip to top navigation\nSkip to side navigation\nSkip to
  content\nSkip to footer\nMaine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp
 \n|\nSearch Maine.gov\nCOURT ALERTS IN EFFECT.\nSee Active Alerts\nHome\n
 →\nNews & Announcements\nSJC invites comments on proposed amendments to 
 the Maine Rules of Evidence to permit parties to authenticate certain elec
 tronic evidence other than through the testimony of a foundation witness. 
 Comments due by September 29\, 2026.\nDate:\n9/8/2026\nThe Maine Supreme J
 udicial Court invites comments on proposed amendments to Rule 902 of the M
 aine Rules of Evidence that would permit a party to authenticate certain e
 lectronic evidence other than through the testimony of a foundation witnes
 s.\nThe proposed amendments are available at the links below.\nAny comment
 s must be filed with the Executive Clerk of the Supreme Judicial Court by\
 n4:00 p.m. on Tuesday\, September 29\, 2026\n.  Comments must be in an ema
 il sent to lawcourt.clerk@courts.maine.gov.  If the comments are in an att
 achment to the email\, the attachment must be a document in native (text-b
 ased and not scanned) portable document format (.pdf).  The Clerk's Office
  will acknowledge receipt of the email via reply email.\nAll comments must
  contain (1) the name and mailing address of the individual submitting the
  comments and (2) the name\, mailing address\, and primary telephone numbe
 r of the organization (if any) on whose behalf the comments are submitted.
   An individual need not be an attorney to submit comments individually or
  on behalf of an organization.\nComments are public documents and may be p
 osted on the Courts website.\nDated: September 8\, 2026\nMatthew Pollack\n
 Executive Clerk\nMaine Supreme Judicial Court\n205 Newbury Street Room 139
 \nPortland\, Maine 04101\n(207) 822-4146\nSupporting documents\nProposed a
 mendments\nPDF version of this notice\nhttps://www.courts.maine.gov/news/a
 rticle.html?id=13345209
URL:https://www.courts.maine.gov/news/article.html?id=13345209
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
